          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Moyal product
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of a linear
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bivector"} Poisson bivector
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \in V \wedge V
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+field"} bivector field
          %p Moyal quantization serves as an intermediate step in quantization of more general situations:
          %p
            Given a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            of Moyal algebras admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
            (non-uniquely) compatible with the algebra structure. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariantly+constant+sections"} covariantly constant sections
            of this Moyal-algebra bundle constitute a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of the symplectic manifold, see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov%27s+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov's deformation quantization
          %p
            With a little care, the Moyal construction applies also to infinite-dimensional Poisson vector spaces such as appear in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} perturbative quantum field theories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            of free field theory (
            %a{:href => "#Dito90"}> Dito 90
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DutschFredenhagen01"} Dütsch-Fredenhagen 01
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov%27s+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov's deformation quantization
            as above yields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interacting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} perturbative quantum field theories
            as constructed via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ), see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
            for more on this.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The Moyal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(V)
            is given on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g \in C^\infty(V)
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi prod
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f \star g \coloneqq prod \circ \exp(\hbar \pi)(f , g)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where in the exponent we regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphism
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V)
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
            in each argument, where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponential
            denotes the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            of iterated applications of this endomorphism, and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi prod
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prod \colon C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V) \to C^\infty(V)
            is the usual pointwise product of functions.
          %p
            This means that given a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x^i\}_i
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            has components
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\pi^{i j}\}_{i j}
            in this basis, the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in the formal parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"}> Planck's constant
            ”) starts out as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi g
                  %mo +
                  %mi ℏ
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mn 2
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo ,
                      %mi l
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi l
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mn 2
                      %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mn 2
                      %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi l
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (f \star g) =
                  f \cdot g
                  +
                  \\hbar
                  \\sum_{i,j} \pi^{i j} \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^i}\cdot \frac{\partial g}{\partial x^j}
                  +
                  \\frac{1}{2}
                  \\hbar^2
                  \\sum_{i,j,k, l} \pi^{k l} \pi^{i j} \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^k\partial x^i}\cdot \frac{\partial^2 g}{\partial x^l \partial x^j}
                  +
                  \\cdots
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#integral_representation Integral representation
          #IntegralRepresentationOfStarProduct.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
                representation of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            %p
              If the functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g
              admit
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+analysis"} Fourier analysis
              (are
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions+with+rapidly+decreasing+partial+derivatives"} functions with rapidly decreasing partial derivatives
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
              is equivalently given by the following
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
              expression:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mi f
                              %msub
                                %mo ⋆
                                %mi ω
                              %mi g
                              %mo )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mfrac
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi det
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ω
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mrow
                                    %mn 2
                                    %mi n
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 2
                                %mi π
                                %mi ℏ
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mrow
                                    %mn 2
                                    %mi n
                            %mo ∫
                            %msup
                              %mi e
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                                  %mfrac
                                    %mn 1
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi i
                                      %mi ℏ
                                %mi ω
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi x
                                %mo −
                                %mover
                                  %mi y
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo ,
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi x
                                %mo −
                                %mi y
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mover
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %msup
                              %mi d
                              %mrow
                                %mn 2
                                %mi n
                            %mi y
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %msup
                              %mi d
                              %mrow
                                %mn 2
                                %mi n
                            %mover
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\left(f \star_\omega g\right)(x)
                    \&amp;=
                    \\frac{(det(\omega)^{2n})}{(2 \pi \hbar)^{2n} }
                    \\int
                    e^{ - \tfrac{1}{i \hbar} \omega((x - \tilde y),(x-y))}
                    f(y)
                    g(\tilde y)
                    \\,
                    d^{2 n} y
                    \\,
                    d^{2 n} \tilde y
                    \\end{aligned}
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Baker58"}> Baker 58
            , see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "star+product#IntegralRepresentationOfStarProduct"} this prop
          %h3#ViaGeometricQuantization Via geometric quantization
          %p
            The Moyal quantization of a Poisson vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,\pi)
            arises equivalently as the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            (
            %a{:href => "#Weinstein91"}> Weinstein 91, p. 446
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GBV"} Garcia-Bondia &amp; Varilly 94, section V
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#EH"} Hawkins 06
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            %a{:href => "star+product#PolarizedSymplecticGroupoidConvolutionProductOfSymplecticVectorSpaceIsMoyalStarProduct"} this prop.
            for the
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong proof
            %em
              %a{:href => "geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids#MoyalQuantizationofPoissonVectorSpace"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids – Examples – Moyal quantization
            for more.
          %h2#References References
          %p The Moyal product was introduced independently in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbrand+Groenewold"}> Hilbrand Groenewold
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the Principles of elementary quantum mechanics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jos%C3%A9+Moyal"}> José Moyal
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Quantum mechanics as a statistical theory
          %p
            The integral expression (prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#IntegralRepresentationOfStarProduct"}
          %ul
            %li#Baker58
              George A. Baker,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Formulation of Quantum Mechanics Based on the Quasi-Probability Distribution Induced on Phase Space
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.109.2198"} doi:10.1103/PhysRev.109.2198
          %p General accounts include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                D. B. Fairlie,
                %em Moyal Brackets, Star Products and the Generalised Wigner Function
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9806198"}> arXiv:hep-th/9806198
                )
            %li
              %p
                Maciej Blaszak, Ziemowit Domanski,
                %em Maciej Blaszak, Ziemowit Domanski
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.0150"}> arXiv:1009.0150
                )
          %p
            The understanding of the Moyal product as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebra"} groupoid convolution algebra
            of the corresponding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
            had been suggested without proof in
          %ul
            %li#Weinstein91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"}> Alan Weinstein
              , p. 446 in P. Donato et al. (eds.)
              %em Symplectic Geometry and Mathematical Physics, (Birkhäuser, Basel, 1991);
          %p and was proven in detail in
          %ul
            %li#GBV
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jos%C3%A9+Gracia-Bondia"}> José Gracia-Bondia
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Varilly"} Joseph Varilly
              = succeed "," do
                %em From geometric quantization to Moyal quantization
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9406170"} arXiv:hep-th/9406170
          %p In a broader context this was reconsidered in
          %ul
            %li#EH
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eli+Hawkins"}> Eli Hawkins
              , example 6.2 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em A groupoid approach to quantization
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.SG/0612363"} arXiv:math.SG/0612363
          %p
            The observation that Moyal deformation quantization applied to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
            yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            quantization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Dito90
              J. Dito,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Star-product approach to quantum field theory: The free scalar field
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/record/303898/"} spire
          %p
            and was amplified in the broader context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            in
          %ul
            %li#DutschFredenhagen01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %em Perturbative algebraic field theory, and deformation quantization
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Longo"} Roberto Longo
              (ed.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mathematical Physics in Mathematics and Physics, Quantum and Operator Algebraic Aspects
          %p
            That moreover the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov deformation quantization
            based on this free field theory star product yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            quantization of interacting field theories is due to
          %ul
            %li#Collini16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Collini"}> Giovanni Collini
              ,
              %em Fedosov Quantization and Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09626"}> arXiv:1603.09626
              )
